WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:08.616
This is Chapter Six, Episode Fifteen: family and family life.

3
00:00:09.166 --> 00:00:13.991
The family has undergone big changes in Denmark through the last hundred years.

4
00:00:13.941 --> 00:00:21.930
Especially since the 1960s, families have been influenced by new cultural currents and forms of living together.

5
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:46.308
Where the family, the nuclear family, kernefamilien, earlier almost always consisted of a man and a woman who were married, and their children, there are today other family types: unmarried cohabiting couples with or without children, couples of the same sex who are married or live in registered partnerships, single parents with children and single people without children.

6
00:00:46.858 --> 00:00:56.770
Marriage between a man and a woman is still the most common form of living together and made up 73 percent of all cohabiting couples in 2026.

7
00:00:56.721 --> 00:01:00.796
But the share of persons who marry has fallen markedly through the years.

8
00:01:00.796 --> 00:01:11.446
For example, 88 percent of all 30-year-old women in 1970 were married, whereas the share in 2026 was only 31 percent.

9
00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:17.022
At the same time both men and women are older than before when they marry for the first time.

10
00:01:17.572 --> 00:01:20.885
That does not however mean that they wait to form couples.

11
00:01:20.835 --> 00:01:29.297
Since the 1970s it has been common, especially among younger people, that couples live together even though they are not married.

12
00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:40.224
Couples who live together without being married, "paperless marriages", do however not automatically have the same legal and financial obligations to each other as married couples.

13
00:01:40.774 --> 00:01:46.912
In Denmark the aim is that homosexuals have the same rights and duties as all other citizens.

14
00:01:46.862 --> 00:01:49.299
It has however not always been so.

15
00:01:49.299 --> 00:01:57.162
Until 1933, sexual relations between persons of the same sex could be punished with a fine or prison.

16
00:01:57.162 --> 00:02:04.637
And only in 1981 was homosexuality removed from the Danish Health Authority's official list of mental illnesses.

17
00:02:04.637 --> 00:02:17.512
But in 1989 Denmark became the first country in the world to give two persons of the same sex the right to enter into a registered partnership, which has largely the same legal effects as a marriage.

18
00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:24.249
Persons of the same sex have since 2012 been able to marry, including to be married in the national church.

19
00:02:24.249 --> 00:02:29.687
The Netherlands introduced in 2001 as the first country in the world this right.

20
00:02:29.687 --> 00:02:35.437
Since 2010, two persons of the same sex have also had the possibility of adopting.

21
00:02:35.437 --> 00:02:40.938
The rainbow flag is a symbol of diversity and equality for LGBT+ persons.

22
00:02:41.488 --> 00:02:45.300
It has also become more common than before that adults live alone.

23
00:02:45.251 --> 00:02:48.900
The number of single adults over 18 has risen sharply.

24
00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:57.925
In 2024, 55 percent of all adults live as singles, that is, they are neither married nor live together with a partner.

25
00:02:57.925 --> 00:03:12.204
The rising share of adults who live alone must be seen, among other things, in connection with it having become more common to get divorced, but it is also influenced by the rising number of elderly people, where one of the spouses has died.

26
00:03:12.754 --> 00:03:17.104
Danish society rests on respect for the individual human being.

27
00:03:17.054 --> 00:03:27.992
This means that men and women have the same rights in political and economic life and in family life with regard to the right to start a family, get divorced and so on.

28
00:03:27.992 --> 00:03:32.129
Children also have rights, even though they are under the age of majority.

29
00:03:32.129 --> 00:03:35.504
The age of majority in Denmark is 18 years.

30
00:03:35.504 --> 00:03:40.379
For example, children under 13 years may not work outside the home.

31
00:03:40.379 --> 00:03:42.666
Parents may not hit their children.

32
00:03:42.666 --> 00:03:52.829
Children who are exposed to violence by their parents, or who are otherwise treated badly, can be forcibly removed by the authorities and placed in care with another family.

33
00:03:52.829 --> 00:04:00.267
When children become 18 years old and thereby of age, they can freely choose how they want to live their lives and with whom.

34
00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:04.542
This means, among other things, that they can enter into marriage.

35
00:04:05.092 --> 00:04:11.117
A man and a woman who wish to marry can enter into marriage in Denmark under certain conditions.

36
00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:17.029
Among other things, both must have legal residence in Denmark and have reached the age of 18.

37
00:04:17.029 --> 00:04:21.579
Marriages in Denmark can only be entered into on a voluntary basis.

38
00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:28.505
It is therefore punishable, strafbart, to try to force someone to enter into a marriage that they do not themselves want.

39
00:04:28.505 --> 00:04:32.784
Forced marriage, tvangsægteskab, can lead to prison, fængsel.

40
00:04:33.334 --> 00:04:44.596
A spouse, whether it is the woman or the man, who does not wish to continue the marriage, has the right to separation for six months and then the right to divorce, skilsmisse.

41
00:04:44.546 --> 00:04:52.996
So divorce is possible even if the other spouse does not want it: the answer to "can a couple be divorced if one party refuses" is yes.

42
00:04:52.996 --> 00:04:57.371
If the couple agree to want a divorce, they can get a divorce immediately.

43
00:04:57.371 --> 00:05:06.114
If they do not agree, a spouse has the right to divorce immediately when there are special circumstances, for example infidelity or violence.

44
00:05:06.664 --> 00:05:14.551
In a marriage the mother and father have joint parental authority, fælles forældremyndighed, over their joint children.

45
00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:21.814
The exams also ask about parents who live together: the starting point is that the father and the mother have it together.

46
00:05:21.814 --> 00:05:26.868
After a divorce the parents continue to have joint parental authority over the children.

47
00:05:27.418 --> 00:05:33.405
If the parents separate, they decide themselves how they will share the responsibility for their joint children.

48
00:05:33.355 --> 00:05:41.068
If one parent wants parental authority alone, it is the court that decides whether the joint parental authority is to continue.

49
00:05:41.068 --> 00:05:44.905
Or whether one of the parents is to have parental authority alone.

50
00:05:44.905 --> 00:05:54.366
Many children live in turns with both parents and get new parents, "papforældre", and siblings, if the parents marry or move in with a new partner.

51
00:05:54.916 --> 00:06:00.816
In Danish: a registered partnership is a registreret partnerskab, and marriage is ægteskab.

52
00:06:01.366 --> 00:06:04.041
Here is your recap for Episode Fifteen.

53
00:06:03.991 --> 00:06:11.491
Marriage is still the most common form of living together, 73 percent of cohabiting couples in 2026.

54
00:06:11.491 --> 00:06:18.803
88 percent of 30-year-old women were married in 1970, and 31 percent in 2026.

55
00:06:18.803 --> 00:06:27.553
Since the 1970s it has been common to live together without being married, but such couples do not automatically have the same obligations.

56
00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:58.166
Until 1933 sexual relations between persons of the same sex could be punished; homosexuality was removed from the list of mental illnesses in 1981; Denmark was the first country in the world with registered partnerships, in 1989; same-sex marriage since 2012, including in the national church; the Netherlands was the first country in the world to introduce it, in 2001; adoption has been possible since 2010.

57
00:06:58.166 --> 00:07:02.728
In 2024, 55 percent of adults live as singles.

58
00:07:02.728 --> 00:07:05.053
The age of majority is 18.

59
00:07:05.053 --> 00:07:10.991
Children under 13 may not work outside the home, and parents may not hit their children.

60
00:07:10.991 --> 00:07:18.053
Marriage requires legal residence and age 18, and must be voluntary; forcing someone is punishable.

61
00:07:18.053 --> 00:07:26.553
Separation is for six months before divorce, unless both agree or there are special circumstances such as infidelity or violence.

62
00:07:26.553 --> 00:07:30.618
Parents have joint parental authority, also after a divorce.

63
00:07:31.168 --> 00:07:33.768
Now it is time for your exam practice.

64
00:07:33.718 --> 00:07:38.630
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

65
00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:46.116
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

66
00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:47.916
Question 1.

67
00:07:47.866 --> 00:07:52.616
How old must you be according to Danish law to marry without any special permission?

68
00:07:52.616 --> 00:07:55.403
Option A: At least 14 years.

69
00:07:55.403 --> 00:07:58.066
Option B: At least 16 years.

70
00:07:58.066 --> 00:08:00.702
Option C: At least 18 years.
[DA] Hvor gammel skal man ifølge dansk lov være for at blive gift uden nogen særlig tilladelse?  A: Mindst 14 år  |  B: Mindst 16 år  |  C: Mindst 18 år

71
00:08:07.252 --> 00:08:10.039
The answer is C: At least 18 years.

72
00:08:09.989 --> 00:08:12.600
It appeared in the June 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Mindst 18 år

73
00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:14.400
Question 2.

74
00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:17.825
Does a homosexual couple have the right to enter into a marriage?

75
00:08:17.825 --> 00:08:19.450
Option A: Yes.

76
00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:21.114
Option B: No.
[DA] Har et homoseksuelt par ret til at indgå ægteskab?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

77
00:08:27.664 --> 00:08:29.451
The answer is A: Yes.

78
00:08:29.401 --> 00:08:33.588
It appeared in the November 2020 and May 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

79
00:08:34.138 --> 00:08:35.475
Question 3.

80
00:08:35.425 --> 00:08:38.588
When did persons of the same sex get the right to marry?

81
00:08:38.588 --> 00:08:41.250
Option A: 1972.

82
00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.838
Option B: 1992.

83
00:08:43.838 --> 00:08:46.134
Option C: 2012.
[DA] Hvornår fik personer af samme køn ret til at indgå ægteskab?  A: 1972  |  B: 1992  |  C: 2012

84
00:08:52.684 --> 00:08:55.146
The answer is C: 2012.

85
00:08:55.096 --> 00:08:57.936
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: 2012

86
00:08:58.486 --> 00:08:59.786
Question 4.

87
00:08:59.736 --> 00:09:05.211
In which year did persons of the same sex get the right to enter into a registered partnership?

88
00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:07.899
Option A: 1955.

89
00:09:07.899 --> 00:09:10.623
Option B: 1972.

90
00:09:10.623 --> 00:09:13.194
Option C: 1989.
[DA] Hvilket år fik personer af samme køn ret til at indgå et registreret partnerskab?  A: 1955  |  B: 1972  |  C: 1989

91
00:09:19.744 --> 00:09:22.469
The answer is C: 1989.

92
00:09:22.419 --> 00:09:25.380
It appeared in the November 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: 1989

93
00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.393
Question 5.

94
00:09:27.343 --> 00:09:33.055
According to Danish law, can a married couple be divorced even if one party does not want it?

95
00:09:33.055 --> 00:09:34.680
Option A: Yes.

96
00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.342
Option B: No.
[DA] Kan et ægtepar ifølge dansk lov blive skilt, selvom den ene part ikke ønsker det?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

97
00:09:42.892 --> 00:09:44.680
The answer is A: Yes.

98
00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:49.056
It appeared in the June 2021 and November 2023 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

99
00:09:49.606 --> 00:09:51.044
Question 6.

100
00:09:50.994 --> 00:09:57.019
Who normally has parental authority when a father and a mother who are married to each other have a joint child?

101
00:09:57.019 --> 00:10:00.231
Option A: The father and mother together.

102
00:10:00.231 --> 00:10:02.231
Option B: The father.

103
00:10:02.231 --> 00:10:04.314
Option C: The mother.
[DA] Hvem har som udgangspunkt forældremyndigheden, når en far og en mor, der er gift med hinanden, har et fælles barn?  A: Faren og moren i fællesskab  |  B: Faren  |  C: Moren

104
00:10:10.864 --> 00:10:14.264
The answer is A: The father and mother together.

105
00:10:14.214 --> 00:10:17.004
It appeared in the June 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Faren og moren i fællesskab

106
00:10:17.554 --> 00:10:18.992
Question 7.

107
00:10:18.942 --> 00:10:24.817
Who has parental authority as a starting point when a mother and father who live together have a joint child?

108
00:10:24.817 --> 00:10:28.029
Option A: The father and mother together.

109
00:10:28.029 --> 00:10:29.929
Option B: The mother.

110
00:10:29.929 --> 00:10:32.046
Option C: The father.
[DA] Hvem har som udgangspunkt forældremyndigheden, når en mor og far, der bor sammen, har et fælles barn?  A: Faren og moren i fællesskab  |  B: Moren  |  C: Faren

111
00:10:38.596 --> 00:10:41.996
The answer is A: The father and mother together.

112
00:10:41.946 --> 00:10:44.640
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Faren og moren i fællesskab

113
00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:46.453
Question 8.

114
00:10:46.403 --> 00:10:50.315
Can you go to prison if you illegally force someone to enter into a marriage?

115
00:10:50.315 --> 00:10:51.940
Option A: Yes.

116
00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:53.610
Option B: No.
[DA] Kan man komme i fængsel, hvis man ulovligt tvinger nogen til at indgå et ægteskab?  A: Ja  |  B: Nej

117
00:11:00.160 --> 00:11:01.948
The answer is A: Yes.

118
00:11:01.898 --> 00:11:04.500
It appeared in the May 2024 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Ja

119
00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.813
That's the end of this part.

120
00:11:06.763 --> 00:11:07.830
Well done.

